          "body": "## Scope\n\nCloses #355. Independent follow-up to the relationship pilot in #354, which remains unmerged. No package behavior changes.\n\nStart with `research/commerce/README.md` for preliminary conclusions and qualitative observations. `SPEC.md` records desired commerce behavior; models, raw SQL probes, emitted DDL, reflected-column diffs, and recorded results sit beside it.\n\n## Findings\n\n- SQLite text preserves decimal digits but sorts lexically and uses floating-point SUM. SQLAlchemy Numeric does not enforce SQLite decimal precision; typed readback can round a value that raw storage retained.\n- MariaDB rounds extra fractional digits under strict mode. snekql's public encoder rejects those application values before binding.\n- UtcDatetime normalizes offsets and rejects naive inputs. Tested SQLAlchemy DateTime(timezone=True) writes lost the offset on both backends.\n- SQLAlchemy declares CHECK and literal server defaults directly; snekql's current scaffold does not expose equivalents. Pydantic bounds remain application validation.\n- Report covers declaration overhead, default timing, error readability, connection policy, and schema flexibility. These are implementation observations, not a usability study or a broad library ranking.\n\n## Evidence and validation\n\n- Eight runs on SQLite 3.50.4 and temporary MariaDB 12.3.2 servers.\n- 128 raw SQL observations, 16 database-rule observations, four timezone-shift observations, plus construction/encoding and selected bound readbacks.\n- `uv run --with-requirements research/commerce/requirements.txt snektest tests research/commerce/test_experiment.py`: 1211 passed.\n- Focused tests, ty, Ruff lint, and Ruff format checks passed after final changes.\n- Extra comparator dependencies are pinned separately; no root lockfile changes.\n- Typing exception is limited to SQLAlchemy's documented Mapped/mapped_column declarations in two research files.\n\nLimitations: local database binaries are recorded rather than provisioned; no ORM session or full snekql runtime benchmark. SQLite storage matching is explicitly DDL-only, without a custom SQLAlchemy codec. No migration workarounds or package fixes concealed unmet commerce requirements.\n",
